======

Free tier: 1 million requests + 400,000 GB-seconds per month.

Region shown in the attached table: US East (N. Virginia).

=====

Think of the 1 million requests + 400,000 GB-seconds as two separate free limits.

1. 1 million requests

A request = one time your Lambda function is invoked.

For example, your chatbot:

User: "What is RAG?"

That sends 1 request to Lambda.

If 100 people each ask 10 questions:

100 × 10 = 1,000 requests

If this happens every day:

1,000 × 30 = 30,000 requests/month

So:

| Chatbot usage                  |     Lambda requests |
| ------------------------------ | ------------------: |
| 1 question                     |           1 request |
| 100 questions                  |        100 requests |
| 1,000 questions                |      1,000 requests |
| 10,000 questions/day × 30 days |       300,000/month |
| Free allowance                 | **1,000,000/month** |


=====

So 300,000 requests/month is still below the 1-million request free allowance. Your attached AWS pricing page states that the free tier includes 1 million requests/month.

======

2. What is 400,000 GB-seconds?

This is slightly confusing.

GB-seconds = Lambda memory × execution time.

For example, suppose you configure your Lambda with 512 MB memory.

512 MB = 0.5 GB

If one chatbot request takes 2 seconds:

0.5 GB × 2 seconds = 1 GB-second

So one request consumes 1 GB-second.

Real chatbot example

Suppose:

Lambda memory = 512 MB
Each question takes = 2 seconds
Users make = 10,000 questions/day

Then:

10,000 × 30 = 300,000 requests/month

And:

300,000 × 0.5 GB × 2 seconds

= 300,000 GB-seconds/month

Your usage:

|                   | Your chatbot | Free allowance |
| ----------------- | -----------: | -------------: |
| Requests          |      300,000 |  **1,000,000** |
| GB-seconds        |      300,000 |    **400,000** |
| Within free tier? |        ✅ Yes |                |


The AWS page explains that Lambda duration is measured in GB-seconds and depends on the memory allocated to the function.

Another example

If your Lambda uses 1 GB memory and takes 5 seconds per question:

1 GB × 5 seconds = 5 GB-seconds/request

If you make 10,000 requests/month:

10,000 × 5 = 50,000 GB-seconds

Still below 400,000 GB-seconds.

=====

Very important for your RAG project

Suppose your architecture is:

Voiceflow → API Gateway → Lambda → Qdrant → LLM

If one user asks:

"Explain the return policy."

Lambda may run for 3 seconds.

If Lambda has 512 MB:

0.5 GB × 3 sec = 1.5 GB-seconds

That's what contributes to the 400,000 GB-second allowance.

But the time Lambda waits for Qdrant/LLM can also contribute to Lambda execution duration, because the function is still executing during that period.

And the LLM API cost is separate from Lambda.

So the simple way to remember it is:

1 million = how many times Lambda runs.
400,000 GB-seconds = how much memory × execution time Lambda uses.

These are monthly free-tier limits, not a limit on how many questions your chatbot can answer in total.

